package main

import "github.com/Obedience-Corp/obey-voice/pkg/voiceagent/brain"

// filledHistoryScript is the prior conversation the filled-history condition
// replays. It is real dialogue about work in this repository — the kind of
// session that actually fills ollama_num_ctx during a day of use — because a
// lorem filler of the same byte count would produce a prompt no tokenizer,
// prefix cache, or attention pattern ever sees in practice.
//
// The script is ordered oldest-first. buildFilledHistory takes the TAIL of it
// that fits the token target, so the retained window is a contiguous, coherent
// conversation rather than a stitched sample.
var filledHistoryScript = []brain.Turn{
	{Role: "user", Content: "Morning. Where did we leave the TTS work last night?"},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "We left it on the Audio8 measurement. The short version is that Audio8 does not clear the bar as shipped. Running it straight through, synthesis came in slower than realtime, so streaming playback would underrun before the first sentence finished. With a prefix cache in front of it and the codec pipelined behind it, the measured end-to-end came out at about one and a half times realtime on one path and two times on the other, but neither run ever reached the two point three we said we needed. The other thing worth remembering is that the vendor runtime cannot execute the vendor's own exported graphs, which is a strange enough finding that I wrote it down twice."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "Right. And that means what for the native provider PR?"},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "It means the provider can land, but the default should not change on the strength of that measurement. The pull request is fine as an option users can select. What we should not do is make it the shipped default and then discover that the first segment costs the most, which is exactly what the in-pipeline numbers showed. In the lab the real time factor looked acceptable, and inside the pipeline it was roughly a third worse, with something like half the wall clock going to dead air at the larger tier. That gap between the lab number and the pipeline number is the reason we now insist on measuring through the real path."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "Okay. Different subject. The voice sessions have been feeling sluggish today."},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "Sluggish how? If it is the gap between you finishing a sentence and me starting to speak, that is a different problem from me speaking slowly once I have started. The first one is dominated by endpointing plus whatever the model takes to produce its first token. The second is synthesis throughput. They have separate fixes and separate benchmarks, so it is worth deciding which one you are describing before we go looking."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "The first one. There is a long dead pause after I stop talking, and sometimes you just give up and say you timed out."},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "Then the suspect is the brain, not the speech stack. The pipeline wraps each turn in a three minute deadline, and when that deadline expires you hear the recovery line instead of an answer. If you are seeing that message at all, some turns are running past three minutes, which is not a slow model, that is a lost reply. The other half of it is context pressure. The context window is set to eight thousand one hundred and ninety two tokens, and as a conversation fills that window the prompt the server has to process before it can emit a single token gets larger every turn."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "So an empty conversation is fast and a long one is slow."},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "That is the shape of it, and it is why measuring only a fresh session would flatter every candidate. A fresh session is the condition you are almost never actually in. By the time a conversation feels natural you have several thousand tokens of history riding in front of every request, and the model has to read all of it before it says the first word. If a model looks fine cold and falls apart warm, the honest verdict is that it fails, not that it passes with an asterisk."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "Which model am I even running right now?"},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "The application level setting points at the twenty seven billion parameter uncensored build, and the persona you have been talking to pins that same model, so that is what has been answering you. Worth knowing that the persona pin and the application setting are two different layers. Some of your other personas pin different models entirely, and one of them points at a mixture of experts build that may behave very differently under the same load, because it activates only a small fraction of its parameters per token even though the file on disk is large."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "Explain the mixture of experts thing to me like I have not thought about it in a year."},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "A dense model runs every parameter it has for every token it produces. A mixture of experts model has many more parameters on disk, but a router picks a small handful of them for each token, so the arithmetic per token is much smaller than the file size suggests. The practical consequence is that the file might be eighteen gigabytes while the work per token resembles a three billion parameter model. It still has to be resident in memory, so it costs you the same amount of RAM, but it can generate several times faster. That is the entire reason it is worth measuring one against a dense model of similar size rather than assuming size predicts speed."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "Does the skills catalog make the prompt bigger?"},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "Yes, and so do the tool schemas. Your system prompt is not just the persona document. It is the persona, plus a block grounding me in the machine and working directory and campaign, plus the catalog of available skills, plus the per turn instruction that keeps replies short and speakable. On top of that, when tools are enabled, the schemas for listing files, reading files, writing files, running commands, searching the web, fetching a URL, and reading a skill all ride along in the request. Every one of those tokens has to be processed before the first word of the answer comes back."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "And the skill router runs before all of that."},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "It does. Each turn, before the chat request is built, your input is embedded and matched against the catalog so that relevant skill instructions can be spliced onto the message. That embedding call is small, but it is synchronous and it happens inside the wait you experience, so it belongs in any honest measurement of time to first token. It is the same cost for every candidate model, so it shifts the whole field rather than reordering it, but leaving it out would understate what the pause actually feels like."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "What about the keep alive setting, does that matter here?"},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "It matters for the first turn after a gap, not for turns inside a conversation. Keep alive is set to ten minutes, so once a model is resident it stays resident through a normal back and forth, and you only pay the load again if you walk away long enough for it to be evicted. A seventeen gigabyte model takes a noticeable amount of time to come back from cold, and a forty two gigabyte one takes considerably longer. That is a real experience, but it is a once per idle gap experience, so it should be reported separately from the per turn numbers rather than mixed into them."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "Okay. Say we did want to change the default. What would convince you?"},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "Numbers taken through the real path, at both an empty and a filled context, with the thresholds written down before the run. Otherwise the thresholds end up being whatever the winner happened to score, which is not a decision, it is a rationalization. I would want first token times at the median and at the worst observed, generation throughput once the reply has started, and proof that no sampled turn came anywhere near the three minute deadline on a short answer. And I would want it stated plainly that none of it says anything about whether the model is pleasant to talk to."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "Fair. What would you not accept as evidence?"},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "A curl against the raw endpoint with a one line prompt. That measures a request nobody makes. The prompt you would be timing has none of the persona, none of the environment block, none of the skills catalog, no tool schemas, and no conversation in front of it, so the prefill it measures is a fraction of the real one. It would make every candidate look good and it would rank them wrongly, because prefill cost is exactly where the large dense models lose. If the measurement does not go through the same code path the conversation goes through, it is not evidence about the conversation."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "Anything else you would want recorded?"},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "The effective request options, so that a reader a month from now can tell whether the run used the same context window and thinking setting you are running today. The exact system prompt length, because that is half of the prefill. Which persona was applied, since the persona overrides the model. The list of models installed at the time, so the exclusions can be checked rather than trusted. And whether the working tree was clean, because a benchmark taken against uncommitted code is a number nobody can reproduce."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "Good. One more thing before we start."},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "Go ahead."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "Do not touch my config while you are measuring, and leave whatever I was running loaded when you are done."},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "Understood. Read the configuration, apply the persona to a copy in memory, and write nothing back. And at the end, put one short generation through the model you were already using so it is resident again and your next conversation does not pay for the benchmark. If a model has to be stopped to get an honest cold load number, that is a load, not a deletion, and nothing gets pulled or removed either way."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "While we are here, remind me what the speech side looks like at the moment."},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "Speech to text is running the Parakeet model through sherpa, which became the default after the A and B comparison, with whisper still available as an explicit choice. Offline mode, with voice activity detection gating the capture: a silence threshold of half a second, a minimum speech duration of a quarter second, and a three hundred millisecond pre roll so the first word is not clipped. On the way out, the synthesis provider is the newer engine with the older one kept as a fallback, and speech speed trimmed slightly below one so it does not sound rushed. None of that is what makes you wait, though. The wait is the model."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "How would I even tell the difference in the moment?"},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "Listen to where the silence sits. If the pause happens after you stop speaking and before anything at all comes back, that is endpointing plus prefill. If I start speaking promptly but the sentences come out haltingly, that is generation throughput or synthesis underrunning. And if I start, stop, and then repeat myself, that is the restatement problem where a model emits its line, fires a spurious tool call, and then emits the same line again on the next iteration. Those are three different failures that all sound like slowness."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "You mentioned spurious tool calls. Is that common?"},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "More common than you would like, and it is very model dependent. A smaller model handed seven tool schemas will sometimes decide that a question about a capital city warrants a web search, or that any question at all warrants loading a skill first. Every one of those decisions costs a full extra round trip: the model generates a tool call, the harness executes it, and then the whole conversation plus the tool result goes back for a second pass. On a slow model that can double the turn. It is not a latency property of the weights, it is a judgment property, but the user experiences it as latency all the same."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "Can we just turn tools off for voice?"},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "You could, and it would make the numbers look better, but it would also remove the ability to read a file or run a command by voice, which is a large part of why the agent is useful at a desk. The better framing is that a model which fires tools indiscriminately on plain conversation is disqualifying itself for the conversational slot, and that is a fact worth measuring rather than configuring away. Keep the tools in the request, count how often each candidate reaches for them unprompted, and let that count be part of the verdict."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "Alright. What about thinking mode, we have that switched off, right?"},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "Off, and deliberately. The reasoning capable models will happily fill a private thinking field and leave the actual message content empty, which the harness then turns into a fallback line about losing a train of thought. For a voice agent that is the worst possible failure, because the user hears a non answer after a long wait. There is a range of levels available beyond the plain booleans, but for conversation the setting stays false so that whatever the model produces is speakable. It also saves the tokens that would have gone into thinking."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "Does turning thinking off hurt the answers?"},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "On short conversational turns, barely. The kind of question you ask out loud while walking around the room does not usually need a hidden scratchpad. On a genuinely hard analytical question it does cost you something, which is the argument for having a separate persona that runs a bigger model with thinking enabled and a longer patience budget, and keeping the everyday persona fast. Two personas, two jobs, rather than one compromise that is mediocre at both."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "That is actually the idea I keep coming back to."},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "It is the right shape. The everyday voice persona should optimize for the feeling of a conversation, which means answering within a beat or two and never dying on a deadline. A second persona can be explicitly slow, and you would invoke it knowing you are asking for depth. The mistake is making the slow one the default and then experiencing every casual question as a wait, which is roughly what has been happening."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "How much of the delay is context and how much is the model being large?"},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "You can separate them by measuring the same model at two context sizes. If a model is fast on a fresh conversation and slow on a full one, the cost is prefill, and prefill scales with how many tokens have to be read before the first one is written. If it is slow at both, the weights are the problem. Most likely you will find the large dense model is slow at both, worse at the second, and that a mixture of experts model of similar file size is dramatically better on the generation half while still paying a real prefill cost."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "And if prefill is the problem, what actually fixes it?"},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "Three things, in order of how much they help. A smaller model, because prefill cost scales with active parameters. A smaller context, because you are reading fewer tokens. And keeping the prompt prefix byte stable so the server's cache can skip re-reading the parts that did not change, which the harness already tries to do by freezing the system prompt and only appending at the tail. That last one is why the system prompt is not rebuilt unless the persona document actually changes, and why history is trimmed with a high and low water mark instead of a sliding window."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "Explain the water mark thing again, I never fully got it."},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "If you drop the oldest message every single turn, then the retained conversation shifts by one message every turn, which means the prefix of the prompt is different every time and the cache never hits. Instead, nothing is dropped until the count crosses twice the retention limit, and then it cuts all the way back to the limit in one go. Between those cuts the retained prefix is byte identical, so the server only has to read the new turn. You pay one large re-read occasionally instead of a full re-read constantly."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "Good. Last thing before you run it. What would make you say the incumbent should stay?"},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "If it cleared the first token target at a full context, held a reasonable generation rate, and never came close to the deadline on a short answer, it should stay, and the sluggishness you have been feeling would have to be something else. I do not expect that, given a twenty seven billion parameter dense model reading several thousand tokens before it speaks, but expectation is not evidence. The numbers decide, and if they contradict me I will say so plainly rather than explaining why the measurement must be wrong."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "One more. How should we think about the three minute deadline itself, is it the right number?"},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "It is generous for conversation and probably too tight for a long tool chain, which suggests it is doing two jobs badly instead of one job well. Three minutes of silence in a spoken conversation is not a wait, it is an abandonment; nobody stands there for three minutes. If the point of the deadline is to stop a wedged model from hanging the loop forever, it could be much shorter for an ordinary turn and the recovery line would land while the user is still present. If the point is to allow a genuinely long agentic sequence to finish, then the deadline should scale with whether tools are actually running rather than being a single flat number. What it should not be is a value nobody chose deliberately."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "So you would shorten it."},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "For a turn that is not running tools, yes, substantially. The value of a deadline is that it converts an infinite hang into a recoverable event, and that value is highest when it fires while the person is still listening. But I would not change it in the same breath as changing the model, because then you cannot tell which change did what. Pick the model on the numbers first, live with it for a few days, and revisit the deadline once the common case is fast enough that the deadline is only catching genuine failures."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "Same question for the context window. Eight thousand, keep or change?"},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "It depends entirely on which model wins. If the answer is a small fast model, you could raise it and buy more conversational memory for very little latency, because prefill on a small model is cheap. If the answer is a large model, you should probably lower it, because every token in that window is paid for at the start of every single turn. Setting it once globally and then pinning different sized models per persona is the part that does not compose: the same eight thousand that is generous for a three billion parameter model is punishing for a twenty seven billion one."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "That is a good point. It should probably be a per persona setting."},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "It should, for the same reason the model already is. A persona is the unit where the tradeoff is actually made, since the persona is what decides whether this identity is meant to be quick or thorough. Today the context window lives at the application level while the model lives on the persona, so switching persona changes half of the pair and leaves the other half behind. That is not urgent, but it is the shape of the eventual fix, and it is worth writing down now while the reason is fresh rather than rediscovering it later."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "Noted. Anything about the skills catalog we should reconsider while we are at it?"},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "The catalog is only names and descriptions, so it is not enormous, but it is paid on every turn and it grows as you add skills. The activated instructions are the larger cost, and those are spliced onto the message rather than the system prompt specifically so the earlier tokens stay stable for the cache. That design is right. What is worth watching is the router's threshold: if it activates a skill on a plain conversational question, you pay for instructions the answer never uses, and you also nudge the model toward reaching for a tool it did not need."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "And that threshold is a config value?"},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "It is, sitting a little above half. Raising it makes activation rarer and turns cheaper, at the cost of occasionally missing a skill that would have helped. Lowering it does the reverse. Like the context window, the right value probably differs between a quick conversational persona and a working one that is actually meant to reach for tools. I would not touch it in this pass, but I would record what fraction of the benchmark turns activated a skill, because if that fraction is high on ordinary chit chat then the threshold is part of your latency story and not just a routing detail."},
	{Role: "user", Content: "Fair enough. Run it when you are ready, and be honest about what you find."},
	{Role: "assistant", Content: "That is the only version of this that is worth doing. I will write the thresholds down first so they cannot be adjusted to fit, measure every candidate the same way through the same code path, report the ones that fail including the one you are currently running, and say plainly where the measurement is weak. Fifteen samples is not a large number, this machine is doing other work, and none of it speaks to whether you enjoy talking to any of these models. Those caveats go in the artifact, not in a footnote nobody reads."},
}

// buildFilledHistory returns the longest contiguous TAIL of the script whose
// estimated prompt (system prompt + history) stays at or under targetTokens.
//
// It takes the tail, not the head, because that is what a real trimmed session
// looks like: the recent turns survive and the old ones fall off the front. The
// window starts on a user turn so the conversation never opens mid-exchange.
//
// sizeFor reports the estimated prompt tokens for a candidate history, using the
// brain's own estimator rather than a second one that would drift from it.
func buildFilledHistory(targetTokens int, sizeFor func([]brain.Turn) int) []brain.Turn {
	script := filledHistoryScript
	// Widen from the end until the next step would exceed the target.
	best := []brain.Turn(nil)
	for start := len(script) - 1; start >= 0; start-- {
		if script[start].Role != "user" {
			continue
		}
		candidate := script[start:]
		if sizeFor(candidate) > targetTokens {
			break
		}
		best = candidate
	}
	return best
}
